From 0ea9471706b8ac03073f36933c185ec1bcadffc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Homies Dev <dev@homies.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 07:02:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] fix: guard the booking flow and consolidate hostel
 authorisation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two separate problems in routes/api/hostels.js, fixed together because
the fix for each is in the same eight handlers.

AUTHORISATION
-------------
Every route verified its token inline, eight separate times. Five copies
checked for role === 'admin'; three did not — GET /rooms,
GET /rooms/:roomId/occupants and DELETE /rooms/:roomId.

Those three were not exploitable, but only by accident: a student token
carries no `hostel` claim, so `decoded.hostel.id` threw a TypeError that
the catch block turned into a 500. The endpoints were protected by a
property access failing rather than by a check. That is the worst kind of
control — it looks deliberate, nothing tests it, and it stops working the
moment someone adds optional chaining while tidying up. It also returned
500 rather than 403, so a misconfigured client could not tell "not
allowed" from "server broken".

All eight now use middleware/hostelAuth.js. The rule lives in one place
and adding a route can no longer mean forgetting it. The hostel id still
comes only from the token, never from the body or params, which is what
keeps one hostel's admin out of another's rooms.

BOOKING INTEGRITY
-----------------
The confirm handler performed no validation whatsoever:

    room.occupants = [studentAId, studentBId];

Assignment, not append. Confirming a pair into an occupied room silently
evicted the pair already in it, while leaving those students' own records
still reading bookingStatus 'confirmed' for that room. Two pairs would
hold the same room with equal confidence and nothing anywhere would show
a conflict. Capacity was never consulted, a student could be confirmed
into two rooms at once, and an occupied room could be deleted outright —
orphaning its occupants into a state no admin screen would ever show
again. A commit message from an earlier session claims a duplicate
booking guard was added; grepping the tree for bookingStatus finds five
references and no guard among them.

Rules now live in lib/booking.js as pure predicates and are covered by
19 cases in tests/booking.test.js, written from the requirement before
the handlers were touched — every one of them fails against the old code.
Each returns a reason, not a boolean, so "Room 4B has 2 of 2 places
filled" reaches the administrator instead of "Failed to confirm booking".

Room status is now derived from occupancy rather than assigned by hand at
each call site, which is how a room could previously be marked 'pending'
with nobody in it. The vocabulary becomes available/partial/full;
statusColor still accepts 'pending' so rows written by the old version
render sensibly instead of falling through to the red unknown branch.

Also here:
- Duplicate room numbers are rejected. Two rooms called "4B" are
  indistinguishable in the confirm dropdown, so an admin cannot tell
  which one they picked.
- Capacity is coerced to an integer. The form sends it as a string from a
  number input, and a string capacity makes every subsequent
  `occupants.length >= capacity` comparison behave unpredictably.
- The switch-room target list filters on remaining capacity rather than
  status === 'available', which previously refused any partly-filled room
  even when it had space.
- GET /matches no longer runs findById inside a nested loop. Both halves
  of a mutual match are necessarily already in the result set, so the
  N+1 was pure waste on every dashboard load.
- SMTP is optional. A transport built from undefined credentials fails at
  send time with an error that reads like a code fault; it now logs what
  would have been sent instead. The confirm response reports whether mail
  actually went out, and the admin is told plainly when a booking saved
  but the notification did not — an admin believing students were
  notified when nothing was sent is the failure worth preventing here.

Rejected: rolling the booking back when email fails. A confirmed room
with no email is a minor problem; an email announcing a booking that was
never saved is a serious one. Email is sent after the write is durable
and cannot affect it.

Does not fix: none of this is tested against a live database. The rules
are unit tested, but the handlers wiring them to mongoose are verified
only by inspection and by the admin UI.
